.TH CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT \- initializer for chttpsvr_config_t with reasonable defaults
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a compound literal that initializes a chttpsvr_config_t
with these defaults:

  host                  = NULL   (dual-stack, every interface;
                                "0.0.0.0" = every IPv4 interface only)
  port                  = 8080
  max_body_size         = 4 MiB  (0 = no limit)
  read_timeout_us       = 60000000   (60 s; 0 = no idle timeout)
  idle_timeout_us       = 60000000   (60 s; 0 = use read_timeout_us)
  stream_read_timeout_us = 30000000   (30 s; 0 = wait forever)
  max_body_read_duration_us = 300000000   (5 min; 0 = no cap on total
                                           read time)
  response_write_timeout_us = 0   (0 = use the value of
                                   stream_read_timeout_us)
  max_response_write_duration_us = 300000000   (5 min; 0 = no cap)
  max_header_read_duration_us = 30000000   (30 s; 0 = no cap)
  max_header_bytes     = 0   (0 = library default, 64 KiB)
  max_connections       = 16384  (0 = unlimited)
  enable_keepalive      = false
  enable_reuseport      = false
  ipv6_only             = false
  tls                   = NULL
  worker_thread_count   = 0   (0 = CPU core count)
  worker_queue_capacity = 0   (0 = 1024 * worker_thread_count)
  min_transfer_rate_bps = 240   (0 = 240; CHTTPSVR_NO_RATE_FLOOR = off)
  min_transfer_rate_grace_us = 5000000   (5 s; 0 = 5 s)
  max_partial_body_memory = 256 MiB   (0 = 256 MiB;
                                       CHTTPSVR_NO_MEMORY_CAP = no limit)
  body_memory_wait_timeout_us = 30000000   (30 s; 0 = 30 s;
                                            CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE = none)
  streaming_thread_count = 0   (0 = worker_thread_count;
                                CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF = share the
                                worker pool)
  streaming_queue_capacity = 0   (0 = 4 * streaming_thread_count;
                                  CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED = no limit)
  streaming_queue_timeout_us = 5000000   (5 s; 0 = 5 s;
                                          CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE = none)

Every timeout and duration field is a uint64_t count of microseconds.
The server keeps each one in whole milliseconds, rounding a value up
to the next millisecond, so a value above 0 never reads as 0.  A value
longer than the server can keep saturates to the longest finite limit
that it keeps for that field, which is never shorter than 24 days, and
never turns into 0 or into "no limit".  CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE, in the
two fields that accept it, is the one value that means no limit.

Every timeout and every resource limit above is finite, so a server
that starts from these defaults is already protected against two
kinds of peer: one that opens a connection and then stalls, and one
that sends a request body, or reads a response, one byte at a time.
You can change each limit independently.  For the fields up to
ipv6_only, a value of 0 keeps its documented meaning for a caller that
asks for it explicitly.  The fields from min_transfer_rate_bps to
streaming_queue_timeout_us read 0 as "use the default", so that a
configuration built with {0}, or with designated initializers that
leave them out, keeps these defenses; each of them is turned off only
through its own named constant.

host follows the listener rules of the Go net package.  NULL or ""
listens on every interface with one dual-stack socket on [::], which
also accepts IPv4 clients through v4-mapped addresses, or on 0.0.0.0
on a host without IPv6.  A literal address listens on exactly that
address.  A host name listens on its first IPv4 address when it has
one, and on its first IPv6 address otherwise, so "localhost" listens
on 127.0.0.1.

host may also be a "unix://path/to/socket" string, which binds a Unix
domain socket at that path instead of a TCP listener; port is then
ignored.  A socket file at that path on which no process listens any
more is replaced.  The start fails, leaving the file alone, when the
path names anything that is not a socket, or a socket on which a live
server listens.

read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us are applied by an idle-timeout
sweep thread that belongs to this module; there is one for the whole
process, shared by every chttpsvr instance, and no OS timer per
connection applies them.  These two fields bound how long a
connection can sit idle between requests, that is, while it waits for
the headers of the next pipelined or keep-alive request.  They do not
bound how long a request can take while a worker thread handles it;
see max_body_read_duration_us for that case.

stream_read_timeout_us bounds the gap between one batch of body bytes
and the *next* while the server reads a request body, buffered or
streaming.  It is separate from read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us,
which reset on any connection activity and so do not protect you
against a client that sends its bytes just fast enough to always beat
them.  After stream_read_timeout_us expires, chttpsvr_req_read returns
-1 and chttpsvr_req_stream_error reports ccol_timed_out; a buffered
route answers 408 automatically.

Note: stream_read_timeout_us itself also resets on every new byte, so
a client that sends a byte or two just before each gap expires defeats
it, just as it defeats read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us.
max_body_read_duration_us closes that loophole by separately capping
the *total* wall-clock time that the server spends reading the body of
one request, however many individual gaps it took to get there.  Its
default is 300000000 (5 minutes), which is large enough for an upload
of max_body_size bytes over a link so slow that the transfer itself is
the bottleneck.  When it expires, it reports the same ccol_timed_out
result as an expiry of stream_read_timeout_us.

A value of 0 in stream_read_timeout_us, max_body_read_duration_us,
response_write_timeout_us or max_response_write_duration_us means
"wait forever".  Such a value does not make an unresponsive connection
a permanent problem: a shutdown of the server still completes in
bounded time, even when a streaming handler really is blocked reading
such a connection.  A shutdown is a call to chttpsvr_destroy or a call
to chttpsvr_engine_stop; a restart with chttpsvr_stop and
chttpsvr_start waits for nothing and cuts nothing off.  The server
achieves this bound by forcibly closing that connection after its own
bounded, graceful wait for the in-flight requests ends.  This happens
only as part of that shutdown sequence and never affects a request
that is still making progress.

response_write_timeout_us bounds the gap between two writes that make
progress while the server sends one response to a client that reads
slowly; it is the write-side equivalent of stream_read_timeout_us.
Progress means the client taking bytes of the response: the send
queue of the socket shrinking, which the sweep checks about once a
second, counts as much as a write that the kernel accepts.  A client
that reads a large response steadily therefore never hits this limit,
however large a send buffer the kernel gives the socket, while a
client that stops reading is cut off between one and two sweep
intervals after the limit passes.  For a TCP client,
min_transfer_rate_bps judges the bytes that reached the client, not
the bytes that the kernel accepted.

max_response_write_duration_us is the write-side equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us.  response_write_timeout_us bounds only each
single call equivalent to write(2), so a client that reads a byte or
two just before the timeout of each such call expires can keep a
response going for as long as it wants.  max_response_write_duration_us
separately caps the *total* wall-clock time that the server spends
sending one response, however many individual calls it took to get
there.  Its default is 300000000 (5 minutes), for the same reasons as
the default of max_body_read_duration_us.  When it expires, the send of
the response fails and the server closes the connection, so the client
sees a truncated response or a reset.

Two further writes are always bounded by a small internal ceiling of 2
seconds, whatever value you set here, including 0: a courtesy
rejection response (400, 404, 405, 408, 413, 500, 501, 503 or 505)
that the server itself generates rather than a handler, and the
interim "100 Continue" line for "Expect: 100-continue".  Both always
have a fixed, small shape, and neither has a legitimate reason to need
longer.  Neither holds a thread while it waits for the peer: a write
that meets a full socket parks the connection, and the ceiling bounds
how long it stays parked.  The interim line of a streaming route is
the exception: chttpsvr_req_read writes it on the thread of the
handler, which waits for the client there just as it waits for the
body, and the ceiling bounds that wait.  A value here below that
ceiling still applies in full.

max_header_read_duration_us is the header-phase equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us.  read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us
measure the gap between one piece of activity and the next, and every
arriving byte resets them, so a peer that keeps sending its request
headers, or its TLS handshake, one byte at a time, forever, without
ever completing them, defeats both.  It never comes near
max_header_bytes either, because it never finishes the header block at
all.  max_header_read_duration_us separately caps the *total*
wall-clock time that one connection can spend in the reactor-owned
phase of one request, which is the TLS handshake, if there is one,
plus the read of the whole header block of that request.  When it
expires, the server closes the connection without sending a response,
because the header block was never complete enough to answer.

The budget is per request, not per connection.  It starts at the first
TLS handshake step for a connection that has one, and otherwise at the
first byte of the header block of the request.  The next request of a
keep-alive connection starts a fresh budget when its own first byte
arrives, so time that the connection spends idle between two requests
is never charged to either one.  The expiry is noticed by the shared
idle-timeout sweep thread, as for read_timeout_us and idle_timeout_us;
that sweep runs once a second and closes every expired connection,
however many there are, so a connection that is past its deadline can
live up to about a second longer than that deadline.

The default of 30 s is generous compared with any legitimate client,
which sends its header block immediately after it connects: even a
full TLS handshake plus several kilobytes of headers over a lossy,
high-latency link takes only seconds.  Raise it for a deployment whose
clients really are slower than that, and set it to 0 to turn the limit
off when something else in front of this server already bounds the
same phase.

max_header_bytes bounds the combined size of the header block of one
request, which is the request line plus every header line.  A request
whose headers exceed it is answered with 400 Bad Request and the
connection is then closed, in the same way as the request parser of
this module reports every other malformed request before routing.
The answer is best effort: a peer that is still streaming its
oversized header block when the server closes may hit a reset before
it reads anything.  Two fixed limits apply alongside it, whatever its
value, with the same 400: the request line and each header line must
end within 8 KiB, CRLF included, and a request holds at most 100
header lines.  See chttpsvr_start(3) for the other requests that the
server refuses before routing.

max_connections bounds how many connections this listener can have
open at the same time.  A finite value bounds how many file
descriptors one listener can hold, so a peer that opens connections
faster than the idle sweep frees them cannot use up the descriptor
limit of the process and take down every other listener with it.
Raise it for a deployment that really serves more simultaneous
connections than the default, and raise RLIMIT_NOFILE to match.  At
capacity, new connections stay pending in the listen backlog of the
kernel instead of being accepted and then rejected: the server does
not call accept(2) again until a connection closes and frees a slot.
The listener stops accepting completely while it is at capacity and
resumes automatically once a slot frees, with a worst case of about
one second between a slot being freed and the listener noticing it.

enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE on every connection that the server
accepts.  enable_reuseport sets SO_REUSEPORT on the listening socket,
which lets more than one chttpsvr instance, for example one per worker
process, bind the same host and port, so that the kernel load-balances
the accepts.  ipv6_only sets IPV6_V6ONLY on an AF_INET6 listener, so
that the listener does not also accept IPv4-mapped connections, and
makes a host name listen on its IPv6 address when it has one.  With
ipv6_only off, a NULL or "" host is always dual-stack, and an IPv6
address or name keeps the system default.  The server applies
enable_keepalive and enable_reuseport on a best-effort basis: a
setsockopt failure does not make chttpsvr_start fail.  None of the
three has any effect on a Unix domain socket listener.

For a buffered route, no worker thread waits out any of these read and
write limits.  A body that stops arriving, or a response that meets a
full socket, parks its connection without a thread, and a later worker
continues it once the socket is ready.  The sweep thread applies the
four limits above to a parked connection once a second, and the
server applies them again when the connection becomes ready.  A
streaming handler reads its body on its own thread, where the limits
bound that wait directly.

min_transfer_rate_bps is the floor on the average rate of a body read
and of a response write, measured from the start of each and judged
once min_transfer_rate_grace_us of it has passed.  It closes a client
that stays just inside every gap limit while moving almost nothing: a
body below the floor gets 408 and a close, a response below it is cut
off and closed, and chttpsvr_req_read() of a streaming handler fails
with ccol_timed_out.  240 bytes a second after 5 s is the minimum data
rate of Kestrel and of IIS.  Time during which the server itself holds
a request back, while it waits for body memory or in the streaming
queue, never counts against the client.

max_partial_body_memory caps the memory that the bodies of buffered
requests on this server hold together, from the first byte that the
server reads until the handler returns.  A reservation covers the
memory that the buffer of a body really holds and lasts as long as
that buffer.  The server frees a body, and returns its reservation, as
soon as its handler returns and before the response goes out, so a
response that waits for a slow reader holds no body; a request that is
refused, cut off or closed frees both at once.  A body with a
Content-Length reserves its whole length before the server reads it,
and its buffer takes exactly that length.  A chunked body reserves its
buffer as the buffer grows, and a growth reserves the old buffer
alongside the new one until the copy ends.  A body that does not fit
waits, without a thread, and nothing reads it and no "100 Continue"
invites it until memory frees.  Waiters are admitted in the order in
which they began to wait.  When every body that holds memory is itself
waiting for more, the oldest waiter goes ahead past the limit, one
request at a time, so the limit is exceeded by less than twice
max_body_size and the server never deadlocks.  A body whose handler is
running is not waiting, so a request that a handler waits for, and
whose body does not fit beside the bodies that running handlers hold,
waits until its deadline.  A waiter that reaches
body_memory_wait_timeout_us gets 503 with "Retry-After: 5" and a
close.

streaming_thread_count sizes the pool that runs streaming handlers,
which the server creates at the first streaming request.  Slow
streaming clients can occupy at most that pool, and never the threads
of buffered routes.  A streaming request that finds every streaming
thread busy waits, without a thread, in a queue of
streaming_queue_capacity entries.  A full queue answers 503 with
"Retry-After: 5" at once, and a request that waits longer than
streaming_queue_timeout_us gets the same answer.

Use CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED for worker_queue_capacity or
streaming_queue_capacity to create a queue with no capacity limit.

After applying the macro, change one field at a time:

  chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
  cfg.port = 9090;
  cfg.worker_thread_count = 8;
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3)
